00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:19.080
Now here's something that most retail investors still get wrong even in 2026, and I have to admit it

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:24.480
cost me real money before I finally figured it out the hard way. Imagine you placed a market order

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:30.760
for an energy ETF last week right when the first Iran-related tanker news hit the wires and volatility

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:36.100
spiked. Your order filled 18 cents higher than the last quoted price you saw on the screen,

00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:42.620
which doesn't sound like much until you do the math. That slippage cost you an extra $162 on what

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:48.220
was only a 900 share position. Money that came straight out of your returns before the ETF even

00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:53.400
had a chance to move. What actually happened behind the scenes is that the bid asks spread on that

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:59.060
ETF widened dramatically from its normal tight three cents all the way out to 22 cents within the first

00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:05.120
45 minutes after the headline, in stressed markets, market makers pull back liquidity to protect themselves,

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:11.140
and that spread becomes a hidden transaction cost that can easily exceed the ETF's entire annual

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:15.720
management fee in a single trade. During the current Iran conflict, we've seen energy and

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:21.820
defense names on both the TSX and NYSE experience average spreads jumping four to seven times their

00:07:21.820 --> 00:07:27.740
normal levels, turning what should be routine trades into expensive propositions. The professionals I

00:07:27.740 --> 00:07:33.260
respect always check the bid asks spread and the 20-day average daily volume before entering any

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:39.660
position larger than 1% of their portfolio. If the spread is running more than 0.25% of the share price

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:45.260
or if current volume is less than 50% of the 20-day average, they immediately switch to limit orders

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:50.760
or simply wait for the next 30-minute window when liquidity tends to improve. The biggest mistake

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:55.640
retail investors continue to make is treating all ETFs as equally liquid regardless of what's

00:07:55.640 --> 00:08:00.600
happening in the news. Instead, the smart habit to build is verifying real-time spread and volume

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:04.960
directly on your broker's advanced quote screen before you ever click the buy button. Doing this

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:09.380
consistently can save you hundreds or even thousands of dollars per year that would otherwise

